// **the case a call binding its own arguments' temporaries got wrong.** `clone()` returns an owning
// `string` with a real buffer behind it, and the `#[implicit]` conversion hands back a `string::view`
// pointing *into* that buffer. so the temporary holding the clone has to outlive the call the view is
// handed to - and while a call bound the temporaries its arguments raised, it did not: the clone was
// released, its str::buf freed, and `show` read the bytes afterwards
//
// nothing about the shape says so from the outside. `clone` and `view` are ordinary library
// declarations, which is why AST::OwnershipPass has no rule naming either one: a call binds nothing,
// and the enclosing statement - the first thing that genuinely outlives the address - does

function show(string::view& $v) : void
{
    echo $v->size;
    echo $v->byte(0);
    echo $v->byte(4);
}

$owned = 'hello world';

// the clone is a temporary; the view borrows into it; the read happens before it is destroyed
show($owned->clone());

// and again through a substring, which owns a second reference to the same buffer
show($owned->sub(6, 5));

echo 1;
